## Lanes: one domain each, no overlap

An agent **writes, comments, suggests and raises trade-offs only inside its lane**. Anything outside it goes to the owning agent with what is needed and why, never done "because it was quicker".

**Refuse and delegate.** Before writing to any path, an agent checks it against its row below. Outside its lane it doesn't write: it stops and hands the work over in its report (the owning agent, what is needed, why), even a one-liner, even when the brief seems to ask for it. A brief that asks for out-of-lane work is the architect's error, and the agent reports it. {{role:architect}} checks this table before every brief.

**A slip is tracked, not thrown away.** When out-of-lane work happens anyway, a sound PR is kept: the owning agent reviews the out-of-lane part. {{role:architect}} logs the slip on the retro issue with a short post-mortem (what happened, why the persona or brief allowed it, the prompt change proposed), and the retro sends the change to {{role:steward}}.

| Agent | Writes (paths) | Hands over |
|---|---|---|
| {{role:architect}} | **nothing in the repo**: design briefs live on work items | every change to the agent that owns its path |
<!-- render: one row per enabled role except the architect, with its resolved lanes (comma-separated, code-formatted) and its hands_over list with themed names. Read-only roles show "**nothing in the repo**: strategies and verdicts go back in the report". -->
| {{owner}} only | `.claude/settings.json` (the harness permission boundary) | — |

**A test belongs to the owner of the code it exercises**, whatever folder it sits in.

**A path not in the table has no owner yet.** {{role:architect}} raises it to {{owner}} as a decision brief and never writes it; {{role:steward}} records the answer here.

**Only {{role:devops}} touches infrastructure and pipelines.** A slice that needs a setting, secret, resource or workflow change gets a task for {{role:devops}} after the contract, never a drive-by edit.

**Contracts.** An interface one lane provides and another consumes (front end ↔ API, port ↔ adapter, event, persisted document, shared artifact) has a versioned contract: types, errors, golden examples. {{role:architect}} designs it as a design brief; the **providing** agent writes the files; the consumer and {{role:security}} review; both sides test against it. The contract also fixes i18n key names and port interfaces, so lanes run in parallel. Changes are backward compatible wherever possible: additive is MINOR; breaking is MAJOR via expand → migrate → contract.

---

## Design, architecture, security and performance are everyone's

File ownership is strict; responsibility for quality is not. Every agent watches for design, architecture, security and performance in its own work and raises what it sees. {{role:architect}} brings the affected agents together, and the **owning** agent makes the agreed fix. {{role:security}} (security) and {{role:devops}} (pipeline and infra impact) are the experts to consult, not the only ones responsible.

