## How work flows

1. **Pitch.** A new idea goes to {{role:product-manager}} (why, what, value) and {{role:product-owner}} (backlog). {{role:architect}} does not plan new scope.
2. **Trade-offs and priority.** The proposal is discussed with {{role:architect}} (feasibility, design impact) and decided by {{owner}}.
3. **Design and contract.** {{role:architect}} posts a design brief on the work item: existing behaviour (from the code and its tests), guards on each path, the contracts, key names and port interfaces. The provider writes the contract; the consumer and {{role:security}} review; lanes start once it is agreed.
4. **Build.** Each agent builds its part against the contract, test first, inside its lane, and **commits its own work** on its own branch with the trailer `{{trailer_example}}`. **Every commit is green**: the agent runs the gates for what it changed before committing; never `--no-verify`. The pre-commit hook is the safety net, not the check.
5. **Review.** See the review gate. A finding goes back to its owner; {{role:architect}} never fixes it.
6. **PR.** {{push_policy_short}} Before every push, every outgoing commit's content and message is grepped (`git log -p origin/main..HEAD`) for keys, tokens, e-mail addresses and identifiers. A hit is removed by rewriting the branch before the push, never by a follow-up commit.
7. **Merge.** {{owner}} approves at merge.

**One agent, one PR.** Each agent works on its own branch, cut from main per work item, and its PR is green on its own. Across lanes, the provider's PR merges first and the consumer branches from main after it, or both build against an agreed contract. The one exception, a change that can't be green split across lanes, ships in one PR with each commit by its owning agent, recorded on the parent item. The docs delta, copy keys and E2E for a change ride in the feature PR as their owners' commits.

**The backlog is the shared environment.** Agents coordinate through work items (issues and their PRs), not through chat. Every agent writes status, what it delivered and what the next task needs on its item. {{role:architect}} keeps items true and starts the next task when its dependencies close.

**Keep the stack current.** Any agent that finds something outdated or improvable in its domain proposes it as a brief with its source and trade-offs; nobody refactors towards it unasked.

