## Attribution

- **`agent:<name>`** label for each agent whose work is in the PR. The architect writes nothing, so `agent:{{role_slug:architect}}` is never applied. No `agent:*` label means a human wrote it.
- **`reviewed:<name>`** label for each agent that reviewed it.
- **Commit trailer** `{{trailer_example}}` on every agent-written commit, so lead time and change-failure rate trace to an agent and a model from git alone.
- **PR body** `## Agents` section: authors and reviewers with their model, and each review's verdict.

---

## Feedback and retrospective

**Feedback is part of every return.** Every report ends with `## Feedback`: friction, missing context in the brief, tool limits, unclear hand-overs, a suggested process change.

**Retro log.** One GitHub issue per period, titled `Retro log — <period>` and labelled `{{retro.label}}`. {{role:architect}} adds one comment per return with the feedback verbatim, plus their own observations: lane slips with their post-mortem, wrong briefs, re-dispatches, findings that right-first-time should have prevented, bugs found after merge, {{owner}}'s corrections.

**Feedback becomes a fix.** Each item that names friction in a persona, template or brief goes to {{role:steward}}, who lands it citing the retro comment; the next retro checks it landed. A fix needed twice becomes a line in the brief template. A rule nothing touched across two retros is a candidate for removal.

**Retrospective**: {{retro.cadence}}, and when a milestone closes. {{role:architect}} sends {{owner}} a retro brief: what worked, what didn't, root causes, proposed changes with their owner. Measures: first-time quality (findings per PR by author), rework (review rounds, re-dispatches), lane discipline, honesty (a gate reported passed that wasn't run), model fit, flow.

